\details{
The main function for advanced data visualization purposes is \code{bgf_plot}.
It is a wrapper that successively calls \code{plot_product_curve}, \code{plot_production_curve}, \code{plot_rel_production_curve}, \code{plot_netProduct_by_Layout}, \code{colplot_yield}  and \code{boxplot_yield}.
It stores the output of each function in a \code{list} which is returned

The second function, that allows advanced data visualization is \code{plot_curve}.
It can be used to draw a line plot from data in the \code{BioGasData}-layer of a \code{BGF}.
In this plot, the 'time' column representing the fermentation/ observation time will be on the x-axis.
The y-axis can be any other column of the \code{BioGasData}-layer and is specified via the 'what' argument.
Furthermore, it is required to provide a grouping variable in the 'color' argument.
If that grouping variable has more than 24 levels, the 'col' argument must be changed as in the default setting only 24 colors are available for plotting.

There exist several wrapper functions, that internally call \code{plot_curve} exist.
These are:

\itemize{
\item \code{plot_product_curve}: Produces a 'product' line  plot. Argument 'what' set to 'product' and argument 'color' set to 'reactor'.  Can be called via \code{bgf_plot} when 'type = "product"' or 'type = "all"'. In the later case, the plot is stored in a \code{list} with the label 'product_curve'.
}

\itemize{
\item \code{plot_production_curve}: Produces a 'production' line  plot. Argument 'what' set to 'production' and argument 'color' set to 'reactor'.  Can be called via \code{bgf_plot} when 'type = "production"' or 'type = "all"'. In the later case, the plot is stored in a \code{list} with the label 'production_curve'.
}

\itemize{
\item \code{plot_rel_production_curve}: Produces an inverted 'rel_production' line  plot. Argument 'what' set to 'c(100-rel_production)' and argument 'color' set to 'reactor'. Can be called via \code{bgf_plot} when 'type = "relProduction"' or 'type = "all"'. In the later case, the plot is stored in a \code{list} with the label 'rel_production_curve'
}

The function \code{plot_netProduct_by_Layout} plots mean net gas curves per layout.
It will extract 'net_product' values from the \code{BioGasData}-layer and calculates a mean for each 'Layout' specified in the \code{metaData}-layer at each 'time' before drawing the line plot.

Can be called via \code{bgf_plot} when 'type = "netProduct"' or 'type = "all"'.
In the later case, the plot is stored in a \code{list} with the label 'net_product_curve'.

The function \code{colplot_yield} produces a col plot with reactor layouts on the x-axis and mean 'yield' values per layout on the y-axis.
It can be used if a yield  summary was transferred to the \code{metaData}-layer using \link{summarize_yield}.
It will generate a colplot with reactor layouts on the x-axis and mean yield values per layout on the y-axis.
With the default settings, fermentations that are marked as 'Excluded' in the \code{metaData}-layer will not be included in the plot.
The standard deviation around the mean value will be calculated and drawn as an \link[ggplot2:geom_errorbar]{errorbar} if possible.

Can be called via \code{bgf_plot} when 'type = "yield_col"' or 'type = "all"'.
In the later case, the plot is stored in a \code{list} with the label 'yield_col'.

The function \code{boxplot_yield} produces a box plot with reactor layouts on the x-axis and and 'yield' values per layout on the y-axis.
The argument 'timep' can be used to specify how many observations will be used to draw the boxes.
In the default setting, 'all', all avalilabel observations for a layout will be used.
Alternatively, to select the final observations 'timep = "max"' can be set.
Furthermore any \code{numeric} that matches a value in the 'time' column of the \code{BioGasData}-layer can be passed to 'timep' to select the specific 'yield' value at that time.

Can be called via \code{bgf_plot} when 'type = "yield_box"' or 'type = "all"'.
In the later case, the plot is stored in a \code{list} with the label 'yield_box'.
